# TEMPLATE — copy to deploy/.env on the box, fill in, `chmod 600`, root-owned.
# deploy/.env is gitignored (the repo-root .gitignore matches `.env` unanchored).
# Every UNCOMMENTED key below is a hard `:?` requirement in docker-compose.yml — with two
# documented exceptions, SEQ_INGEST_API_KEY and SEQ_ADMIN_PASSWORD, which are `:-` so a
# missing value fails the seq container alone rather than the hourly reconcile's entire
# apply. Compose otherwise
# refuses to start without it rather than starting with a default nobody chose. The
# commented-out keys are the exception and are optional by construction — ACME_CA,
# ACME_CHALLENGE_MODE, IMAGE_TAG, MIGRATE_ALLOW_SCHEMA_AHEAD, the EMAIL_* block,
# SEQ_SERVER_URL, SEQ_MINIMUM_LEVEL, the AUTH_* block and
# ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_INITIAL_ADMIN_EMAIL each carry a default in the compose file. Where a
# default exists it is stated beside the key, because "optional" without the
# resulting behaviour written down is how a value nobody chose becomes production.
#
# NOT selected here: COMPOSE_FILE. The stack is always selected by path
# (`docker compose -f /opt/jobbliggaren/deploy/docker-compose.yml ...`) because
# COMPOSE_FILE in a project's .env overrides file resolution and the compose guard
# is structurally blind to it (#1217).

# --- Edge -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The hostname the certificate is issued for. K1: dev.jobbliggaren.se first; the
# apex stays parked at Strato.
SITE_HOST=dev.jobbliggaren.se

# Contact address Let's Encrypt uses for expiry warnings.
#
# MUST be the controller's own address. Never a shared or user-facing mailbox: with Cloudflare
# gone (Klas decision K3, ADR 0050 `Amendment 2026-08-04` §3), Caddy talks to Let's Encrypt
# directly, so ISRG (USA) is the only new party the edge gained. The controller's own address is
# the controller's own data and owes no processor entry -- a user-facing address here would make
# ISRG a recipient of user data and reopen Chapter V. This line prescribes; it does not measure.
# The value lives only on the box, and #1199 could not read it (security-auditor 2026-08-09).
ACME_EMAIL=

# Leave unset for production issuance. Set to
# https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory while proving the challenge
# path — production limits are 5 duplicate certificates per week and 5 failed
# validations per hour, and a shadowed challenge burns them.
#ACME_CA=

# Which ACME challenge type Caddy is allowed to use. Leave unset: the default `both` resolves
# to an EMPTY snippet, i.e. Caddy's own behaviour with both challenges enabled.
#
# The two other values exist to PROVE issuance, never to run on. Caddy runs HTTP-01 and
# TLS-ALPN-01 and chooses between them adaptively, so a certificate obtained with both enabled
# says nothing about which path carried it — and the runbook is blunt about the consequence:
# "Never configure TLS-ALPN-01 away without having proven HTTP-01 for real." Force one at a
# time, on the STAGING directory, and read the issuance log line that names the challenge type.
#
#   ACME_CHALLENGE_MODE=http01   # TLS-ALPN-01 disabled — verification-log row 5
#   ACME_CHALLENGE_MODE=alpn01   # HTTP-01 disabled    — verification-log row 6
#
# THE KEY IS PERMANENT, and a follow-up that deletes it after cutover would be a defect
# (CTO 2026-08-05). The certificate renews forever, and the renewal path can be broken later
# by a firewall change, a Caddy upgrade or a DNS move. This is a diagnostic instrument; without
# it the next incident starts by re-deriving and re-deploying the mechanism on a box that is
# down at that moment.
#ACME_CHALLENGE_MODE=both

# K2 admission gate. Generate the hash with (one line):
#
#   sudo docker run --rm -it caddy:2-alpine caddy hash-password --algorithm bcrypt --bcrypt-cost 11
#
# NO `--plaintext`, DELIBERATELY, and the omission is the whole point. `--plaintext '<password>'`
# puts the K2 password in argv, and argv reaches THREE durable sinks, not one:
#
#   1. the persistent journal — `docker` needs `sudo` on this box and `sudo` logs every command
#      it runs, arguments included. This is #1343's defect class one file over.
#   2. the operator's shell history — independent of sudo, and it survives the session.
#   3. the terminal scrollback — likewise, and it is the one people paste from.
#
# Naming all three matters because the sudo half is the only one that is box-specific: a reader on
# a host where docker needs no sudo would otherwise read this note as not applying and reinstate
# the flag. Omitting the flag makes `hash-password` prompt on stdin with echo off, where the value
# reaches none of the three. `-it` is required for that prompt. (Corrected 2026-08-16; the old form
# stood here through the whole #1343 sweep, because that sweep looked for the master key rather
# than asking what else reached argv the same way.)
#
# COST 11, NOT THE DEFAULT 14, and it is a decision rather than a shortcut (Klas
# 2026-08-05). Caddy caches only SUCCESSFUL comparisons, so a wrong password pays the
# full hash every time, which makes this gate an unauthenticated CPU amplifier against a
# box with no CDN in front of it. Cost 14 defends against offline cracking of a leaked
# hash; YOUR hash is never published. It lives in `deploy/.env` on the box and, measured,
# a second time in Docker's own container state — both root-only, and anyone who can read
# either already has root. The default bought a threat we do not have and paid for
# it in availability we do need.
#
# ARGON2ID IS REFUSED, and it is refused at the CLASS rather than at a threshold, even
# though Caddy calls it recommended and bcrypt legacy. Its default is m=47104, i.e. 46
# MiB allocated PER VERIFICATION. Any per-request memory allocation on an unfiltered,
# unauthenticated edge is a memory-exhaustion primitive, and the attacker picks the
# concurrency — so no value of m makes it safe here, and lowering m does not rescue it.
# Raising the edge cap would be the wrong direction entirely: it admits the primitive
# and then feeds it.
#
# WRAP THE HASH IN SINGLE QUOTES. Store the plaintext in a password manager, never here.
# The reason matters because the damage does not look like damage: compose interpolates
# `.env`, and where a `$` is followed by a character that can START a variable name it
# eats the longest run of [A-Za-z0-9_] and substitutes it away. That is why `$2a` and
# `$11` survive - a name cannot start with a digit - while a bcrypt salt beginning with a
# LETTER loses its leading run up to the first `.` or `/`.
#
# IT IS THE COMMON CASE, NOT AN EDGE CASE. The bcrypt alphabet is `./A-Za-z0-9`, so 52 of
# 64 possible first salt characters are letters - about four in five hashes are mangled.
# Measured on Compose 2.40.3, 2026-08-05, over 20 real cost-11 hashes: 16 mangled, 4
# intact. The version belongs to the claim: interpolation semantics are what this is about.
#
# The mangled form is the danger, and it is worth seeing once. This pair is reproducible:
# the plaintext is the literal string `losenord-6`, generated for this measurement and
# used for nothing, so the hash below is worth exactly nothing to anyone who cracks it.
#
#   in   $2a$11$Yq74328Mrsj/hVw45XkEi.37o3Y.rENU2ySYye1b2bLA/jpzdZ7i6
#   out  $2a$11/hVw45XkEi.37o3Y.rENU2ySYye1b2bLA/jpzdZ7i6
#
# Note what the output is NOT: it is not a short, obviously broken `$2a$11`. It is long
# and plausible, so an operator eyeballing it sees a hash. Compose warns and exits 0,
# `:?` does not fire, and the site then 401s on the correct password. Single quotes stop
# interpolation and make the outcome the same every time.
#
# YOUR hash never goes in this file. This repo is public.
BASIC_AUTH_USER=
BASIC_AUTH_HASH=''

# --- Application secrets: NOT IN THIS FILE ----------------------------------
# The field-encryption master key and the three pseudonymisation peppers were removed
# from here by #198 (ADR 0050 gate B-1: the master key must never be plaintext on disk).
# They are written to /run/jobbliggaren/secrets/ — tmpfs, RAM-backed — by the operator
# after every boot, and reach api and worker as a read-only bind mount. There is no
# encrypted-at-rest copy on this disk, deliberately; the host has no TPM, so a sealed
# blob and its unsealing key would travel together in any disk snapshot.
#
# Procedure, including the per-boot re-injection: docs/runbooks/master-key-ops.md.
#
# ROTATABILITY IS PER SECRET, AND THE OLD ONE-SENTENCE RULE HERE WAS WRONG. This block used
# to say "GENERATE ONCE, then never rotate" about all four values at once. That is a template
# sentence covering four different truths, and it was taken for a measurement — it is what
# led an earlier design to move the peppers byte-identically when three of the four could
# simply be replaced. Per secret:
#
#   FieldEncryption master key — ROTATABLE. Re-wrapping the stored per-user DEKs is a bounded
#     operation (#198 gate M-3); field data is never touched. The runnable arm
#     (`migrate rewrap-master-key`) ships in #198's second PR and is NOT yet code. Note that
#     with zero rows in user_data_keys there is nothing to re-wrap at all, so a rotation today
#     is simply new bytes.
#
#   AuditPseudonymization pepper — ROTATABLE AT ANY TIME, including against a full database.
#     The HMAC is written into a write-only jsonb audit field that no read path ever matches
#     against, and the partitions age out. The DI registration already calls it
#     "rotation-tolerant".
#
#   CvReviewFingerprintPseudonymization pepper — rotatable while `resume_finding_statuses` is
#     EMPTY. The fingerprint is recomputable, but rotating it makes every stored
#     target_fingerprint mismatch, so every finding a user marked Ignored or Resolved silently
#     reverts to Open.
#
#   CompanyWatchPseudonymization pepper — ROTATABLE ONLY WHILE `company_watches` HAS NO ROWS,
#     and the window closes at the FIRST row, not at "first real data". The backfill destroyed
#     the plaintext organisation number in place, so tokens cannot be recomputed under a new
#     pepper — not expensively, but mathematically. One test user following one company locks
#     this pepper permanently.
#
# Losing any of the four has the same effect as rotating it after that point: the data it
# protects becomes unreadable or unmatchable. Escrow accordingly.

# --- Database roles ---------------------------------------------------------
# Operator-provisioned; `migrate init` creates the roles with these passwords and
# the application then connects as jobbliggaren_app / jobbliggaren_worker.
#   openssl rand -base64 24
POSTGRES_MASTER_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_MIGRATIONS_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_APP_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_WORKER_PASSWORD=

# --- Release ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The rollback control, and it rolls back CODE ONLY. Unset means `latest`. To roll
# back, pin the previous published tag (`sha-<short>`) and re-run the reconcile unit
# — seconds, and it is the primary rollback path for api, worker, web and caddy.
#
# IT IS NOT A SCHEMA ROLLBACK, AND SINCE #1236 `migrate` REFUSES TO PRETEND IT IS.
# There are no down-migrations, and this repo has measured cases where re-running an
# older migrate against a newer schema destroys data irreversibly. So when
# __EFMigrationsHistory holds migrations the pinned image's assembly does not
# contain, `schema` mode refuses before MigrateAsync — exit 3 (pure backwards pin,
# overridable below) or exit 4 (diverged history, never overridable) — and api and
# worker stay down, fail-closed. The refusal in `docker logs jobbliggaren-migrate`
# names the IDs and the three exits; anatomy and procedure:
# docs/runbooks/vps-deploy-stack.md §3a. Across a migration boundary, treat a
# backwards pin as a restore problem, not a deploy problem.
#
# A Netcup snapshot is NOT deploy rollback; its role is before a migration, once real
# user data exists — which is exactly the boundary the tag cannot cross.
#IMAGE_TAG=sha-0000000
#
# The deliberate old-code-on-newer-schema consent (#1236, exit-3 states only). THE
# VALUE IS THE EXACT REFUSED MIGRATION-ID SET — comma-separated, printed verbatim by
# the refusal message — NEVER `1`. A leftover boolean would bless the NEXT accidental
# backwards pin months later; an ID set stops matching the moment a different pin
# produces different IDs, so a forgotten value refuses again instead of waving
# through. Set it, re-run the reconcile unit (migrate then skips MigrateAsync and
# certifies nothing — the compatibility judgment is yours), and REMOVE it when the
# incident is over: a green run with it still set logs a nudge to do exactly that.
#MIGRATE_ALLOW_SCHEMA_AHEAD=

# --- Outbound email (#183) --------------------------------------------------
# ALL OPTIONAL, AND THE DEFAULTS ARE THE CURRENT BEHAVIOUR. Leaving this whole block
# commented out yields Email__Provider=Console, which outside Development/Test resolves to
# NullEmailSender: the application finds its recipients, builds its messages, and delivers
# them nowhere, silently. That is the DEFAULT this block describes — it is no longer what the
# box does: since 2026-08-16 the box's own .env carries EMAIL_PROVIDER=Scaleway and the arm
# sends for real, while release-checklist.md §2.5 point 1 is not green. Leg (e) records what
# that means; do not re-derive it here and do not read this line as a measurement of the box.
#
# SETTING EMAIL_PROVIDER=Scaleway IS THE PROD FLIP. It is gated by
# docs/runbooks/release-checklist.md §2.5 — point 1 is not green, and its legs' statuses are
# read in the point itself, never from a summary — and ADR 0124 states that CC never
# performs it. Do not uncomment it to "test something".
#
# `Ses` AND `Resend` ARE NOT OLDER SPELLINGS OF THIS — THEY ARE BOOT REFUSALS. Both arms were
# deleted rather than repointed (#183 E1, b71c14de; ADR 0124 before it), and AddEmailSender's
# switch ends in `else throw`, so either value crash-loops api and worker. If you are following
# an instruction that names one, the instruction is stale. `--check` reports it as INVALID.
#
# NO SETUP RUNBOOK EXISTS RIGHT NOW, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT. The SES
# one was deleted with its provider (#183 E2, Klas decision 2026-08-15): a stale operational
# runbook is more dangerous than none, because it reads as current. The Scaleway console
# procedure — domain verification, DKIM, key rotation — is written in E4 out of the steps Klas
# actually performs at the flip; nobody invents them here.
#EMAIL_PROVIDER=Scaleway
#
# Always explicit, and `fr-par` is the only accepted value: ScalewayClientRegistration holds an
# allow-list, and Transactional Email runs in that region alone (measured 2026-08-15). Two reasons
# it is a hard allow-list rather than a default: the region is interpolated straight into the
# endpoint URL, and it decides which jurisdiction mail leaves from.
#
# A WRONG VALUE IS A BOOT REFUSAL, NOT A 404. EnsureAllowedRegion runs inside
# AddScalewayEmailClient — registration, not first send — so api and worker refuse to start and
# say why. That is the fail-open this allow-list closes: without it, `fr-pars` would build a
# perfectly well-formed URL, pass every null check, and fail first as a 404 on the first live
# send, in production, after a deploy. AddEmailSender likewise refuses to register with this value
# absent.
#EMAIL_SCALEWAY_REGION=fr-par
#
# Pointers to files, NOT the credentials themselves — the `_FILE` seam, same as the master
# key. A pointer naming a path that cannot be read THROWS at boot, so leave these commented
# until the files exist.
#
# TWO SECRETS, ROTATED INDEPENDENTLY. `SecretKey` is rotated at the Scaleway console; `ProjectId`
# changes only if the project does. Rotate one without touching the other — the reasoning has one
# home you can open from the box, jobbliggaren-inject-secrets.sh's SCALEWAY_SECRET_KEYS.
#
# jobbliggaren-inject-secrets.sh WRITES THESE FILES. It prompts for them when EMAIL_PROVIDER
# above is Scaleway, when either pointer below is set, or when it is run with
# JBL_INJECT_SCALEWAY=1 (#183). Conditional rather than unconditional because listing them always
# would demand credentials at every boot and put a permanent MISSING on
# jobbliggaren-secrets-present.service for a state that is correct, while omitting them entirely
# would leave the flip unable to place a secret at all.
#
# INJECT BEFORE YOU EDIT, and the order is not cosmetic. Setting EMAIL_PROVIDER=Scaleway or
# uncommenting a pointer while the files are absent is ITSELF a boot refusal — AddEmailSender
# throws at registration, and an unreadable pointer throws on read — so editing this file first
# takes the box down, and the injection then happens under an outage. The order that avoids it:
#
#   1. sudo JBL_INJECT_SCALEWAY=1 /opt/jobbliggaren/deploy/systemd/jobbliggaren-inject-secrets.sh
#   2. set EMAIL_PROVIDER=Scaleway, uncomment the two pointers below, EMAIL_SCALEWAY_REGION above
#      AND EMAIL_SCALEWAY_KEY_EXPIRES_AT below — the expiry date is REQUIRED under Scaleway and
#      belongs in THIS edit, not a later one. Setting the provider without it leaves --check
#      failing on a box whose stack is perfectly healthy, which is the always-lit state the check
#      itself is designed to avoid. Nothing enforces the order; this step list is the enforcement.
#   3. restart api and worker
#
# `--check` verifies afterwards that every file and every line above is PRESENT, and names any one
# that is not. It reads only TWO values — EMAIL_PROVIDER's and the expiry date's — so a misspelt
# pointer path or a wrong region passes it and is refused at registration on the next start
# instead. The rule is "no value that has a second reader": pointers and region are refused inside
# AddScalewayEmailClient, while an expired key is refused nowhere, which is why that one exception
# exists.
#EMAIL_SCALEWAY_SECRET_KEY_FILE=/run/app-secrets/Email__Scaleway__SecretKey
#EMAIL_SCALEWAY_PROJECT_ID_FILE=/run/app-secrets/Email__Scaleway__ProjectId
#
# THE KEY'S DEATH DATE — NOT A SECRET, AND REQUIRED UNDER Scaleway (#183 E4). Scaleway caps an API
# key's life at one year and offers no instance-role equivalent, so the SecretKey above dies on a
# date, and the reasoning for why that needs a reader at all has ONE home you can open from the
# box: jobbliggaren-inject-secrets.sh's EXPIRY_NOTICE_DAYS header. Do not restate it here.
#
# WHAT THIS LINE DOES, IN TWO HALVES (senior-cto-advisor, binding 2026-08-16):
#   EXPIRED, unset, or a value that is not YYYY-MM-DD -> exit non-zero, into systemctl --failed.
#   Mail is dead NOW in each of those, so it belongs on the fault surface.
#
#   Inside the notice window -> a journal line and exit 0. The box stays GREEN. A lead time on a
#   LATCHING surface would suppress the transition every other heartbeat predicate needs in order
#   to notify, so a maintenance reminder would cost real detection. The advance-warning half is a
#   calendar obligation and belongs to #1267, whose reminder is NOT built — which is why the
#   notice line says so out loud instead of implying someone will chase you.
#
# Read the date in the console (IAM -> API keys -> the key -> Expiration) and write it as
# YYYY-MM-DD. Relative forms are rejected on purpose: `date` would happily parse `nextyear`, and
# it would re-resolve against the clock every run, so the notice could never fire.
#
# ROTATING THE KEY MEANS UPDATING THIS LINE IN THE SAME PASS — it is the one part of the rotation
# nothing else enforces. #198 owns rotation; the current key's date and provenance are recorded in
# docs/runbooks/master-key-ops.md.
#EMAIL_SCALEWAY_KEY_EXPIRES_AT=2027-08-16
#
# ONE-TIME CLEANUP OWED FROM THE SES ERA, and it is an instruction rather than a claim about
# what is there. AWS refused production access permanently (2026-08-14), so the SES IAM user's
# static access keys have no remaining purpose: **revoke them at AWS** — Klas's action, #183
# decision 3. The principal is the IAM user `jobbliggaren-ses` with policy
# `jobbliggaren-ses-send` in account `710427215829`; revoke the USER's static access keys, and
# note that a measurement taken with the SSO admin role answers a different question than the one
# about the key that reaches configuration.
#
# On the box, `/run` is tmpfs and every reboot empties it, so the files below may well already be
# gone; check rather than assume, and remove any survivor so no stale credential is mounted into
# api and worker. **`find -delete`, never a glob:** the directory is `0710 root:<container-gid>`
# and the operator is not root, so a shell glob expands in the UNPRIVILEGED shell before `sudo`
# ever runs, matches nothing, and is passed through literally — `rm -f` then exits 0 having
# deleted nothing, which is a remedy that reports success and changes nothing.
#
#   sudo ls -l /run/jobbliggaren/secrets/ | grep -i '__Ses__'   # expect: no output
#   sudo find /run/jobbliggaren/secrets -maxdepth 1 -name 'Email__Ses__*' -delete
#
# EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS is deliberately NOT a variable: the sender must lie under the domain
# identity verified at the provider, and p=reject makes a wrong sender silence rather than a
# bounce — so the compose file carries the literal, and there is no knob whose only
# reachable non-default values are wrong ones.
#
# Email__BaseUrl is NOT a variable here either: compose derives it from SITE_HOST, because
# the links in confirmation mail must point at this box and the code default is
# http://localhost:3000.

# ==================================================================================
# Structured log sink — #1175 (B), Seq on the internal network
# ==================================================================================
#
# UNSET LEAVES BOTH HOSTS CONSOLE-ONLY, EXACTLY AS BEFORE. AddJobbliggarenLogging attaches
# the Seq provider only when `Seq:ServerUrl` is non-empty, so an absent value here is not a
# half-configured sink — it is the pre-#1175 behaviour, unchanged.
#
# THE PORT IS THE INGESTION LISTENER, NOT THE UI. Seq serves its UI and query API on 80 and
# accepts ingestion on 5341. Pointing the app at 5341 keeps the query API out of the app's
# configuration — it is NOT network isolation, since containers on the same bridge reach each
# other by default (measured 2026-08-11). What bounds a compromised container is that the query
# API answers 401 without authentication; see the compose file's seq comment.
#
# THE THREE KEYS HAVE AN ORDER, AND THIS ONE IS LAST — which is why it ships commented out while
# the other two ship empty. Setting it is what ATTACHES the provider, so a fresh copy with this
# active and the other two empty points both hosts at a Seq that refuses to start (no admin
# password) with no ingest key to authenticate with. Order: SEQ_ADMIN_PASSWORD, then seq up, then
# the ingest key created inside Seq, then this. log-sink.md §3 is the procedure.
#SEQ_SERVER_URL=http://seq:5341
#
# An INGEST-ONLY API key, created in Seq after first run (Settings -> API Keys). Not a
# root-survival control — root on this box reads the volume directly — but it stops a
# compromised application container from reading or deleting the corpus.
SEQ_INGEST_API_KEY=
#
# Read ONLY on Seq's first run against an empty volume. After that the password lives in
# Seq's own store and THIS VALUE STOPS BEING THE SOURCE OF TRUTH — change it in Seq, not
# here. Seq 2026.1 refuses to start without it (measured 2026-08-11), which is the
# fail-closed default that makes #1198's unauthenticated-for-months shape hard to recreate.
#
# IT BECOMES THE SOURCE OF TRUTH AGAIN IF THE VOLUME IS LOST. "Stops being" is true only while
# seq_data survives: destroy or lose the volume and the next start is a first run, which reads
# THIS value. A stale value left here is therefore not inert — it is the password to a fresh Seq,
# and the one an operator will not think to look for. Rotate it here when you rotate it in Seq.
SEQ_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
#
# Optional. Defaults to Information in the compose file.
#SEQ_MINIMUM_LEVEL=Information

# --- Registration gate (#734) -----------------------------------------------
# ALL OPTIONAL, AND THE DEFAULTS ARE THE CURRENT BEHAVIOUR. Leaving this block commented
# out yields Auth__RegistrationsOpen=false, which is what the box does today: registration
# answers 503, so no NEW account can be created (existing accounts and their logins are
# unaffected). Write `true` or `false` — the .NET binder rejects `1`/`yes`/`on` and the
# boot fails loudly rather than guessing.
#
# WHAT ENFORCES THE COMBINATIONS IS AuthOptionsValidator, NOT THIS FILE. It exempts
# Development and Test and applies everywhere else, so on this box its refusal is a boot
# failure naming the offending key. Two ways to get one:
#
#   open + AUTH_REQUIRE_EMAIL_CONFIRMATION=false  -> refused. An open gate without
#     confirmation mints accounts bound to addresses nobody proved they own.
#   open + confirmation, but EMAIL_PROVIDER not delivering (Console outside
#     Development resolves to NullEmailSender) -> refused. The account would be created,
#     login blocked on EmailConfirmed, and the activation link would reach nobody.
#
# So THE EMAIL FLIP COMES FIRST — see the outbound-email block above and follow its own
# inject-before-you-edit order, then set the two keys here in the same visit.
#
# Verify after the restart, and read the gate's own line rather than inferring from a
# healthy container:
#
#   docker logs jobbliggaren-api 2>&1 | grep 'Registration gate'
#
# An open gate outside Development logs at WARNING and repeats every boot by design
# ("Registration gate: OPEN outside Development; email confirmation: REQUIRED"); that
# recurrence is the posture being alertable, not a fault.
#
# CLOSING THE GATE IS ONE LINE, NOT TWO: comment out AUTH_REGISTRATIONS_OPEN — then RE-CREATE,
# never `docker restart`:
#
#   cd /opt/jobbliggaren/deploy && sudo docker compose -f docker-compose.yml up -d --pull never api
#
# The substitution above happens at container CREATION, so `restart` re-runs the process against
# the env it already holds: the gate stays OPEN while this file says closed, and the command
# reports success. Then read the gate's own line and expect CLOSED — commenting the line out is
# not the confirmation, the log line is (see docs/runbooks/registration-gate.md step 10).
#
# Accounts and logins survive a closed gate. LEAVE AUTH_REQUIRE_EMAIL_CONFIRMATION=true —
# both validator rules only fire while the gate is open, so with it closed a `false` here
# is accepted SILENTLY, at Information level, and it disables the EmailConfirmed login gate
# for any account that never followed its link. That is one of the two wrong postures this
# file cannot make loud — the other is a standing value in the admin knob below.
# Procedure, including how the first accounts are created: docs/runbooks/registration-gate.md
#AUTH_REGISTRATIONS_OPEN=true
#AUTH_REQUIRE_EMAIL_CONFIRMATION=true
#
# --- DEV TOOLING (REMOVE BEFORE LAUNCH) -------------------------------------
#
# Makes the owner-scoped onboarding reset reachable on this box, so the welcome setup can be
# re-tested here. ONE variable feeds BOTH services (api + web) — set together or not at all:
# with only the api half the endpoint exists and nothing renders, with only the web half the
# button renders and every press is refused.
#
# It is DESTRUCTIVE. It clears the caller's CV, uploaded originals, saved job ads, recent
# searches, graded matches and matching choices. The account, applications, company watches and
# saved searches survive. It is owner-scoped, so it can only ever reach the caller's own data.
#
# The flag is visible to EVERY logged-in account on this box, not just the operator's.
# Turn it off again when you are done, and remove it entirely before real users:
# docs/runbooks/release-checklist.md 2.7.
#DEV_TOOLS_RESET_ENABLED=true
#
# --- Job-ad ingestion -------------------------------------------------------
#
# Turns on the Platsbanken sync. Default in docker-compose.yml is `false`, and the shipped
# Production overlay carries IngestEnabled=false as well, so an absent key here ingests
# nothing. Setting it to true lands JOB ADS AND DECLARED RECRUITER CONTACTS
# (application_contacts -> AdContact: name, role, email, phone), on the registered
# ten-minute cron, plus a full snapshot backfill at 02:00 UTC. A third writer obeys the same
# flag and is not on a cron at all: JobAdRefetchBackfillRunner, which carries the same contacts.
#
# WHAT AUTHORISES SETTING IT is Klas's explicit written GO, given 2026-08-17 and recorded with
# its adjudicator, date and place in docs/runbooks/release-checklist.md 2.6 point 3.5. Neither
# this key nor the compose default is permission; they are the mechanism the GO operates.
#
# SAME CREATION-TIME TRAP AS THE AUTH GATES ABOVE: the substitution happens when the container
# is CREATED, so `docker restart` re-runs the process against the env it already holds and
# reports success while the gate is unchanged. Re-create instead:
#
#   cd /opt/jobbliggaren/deploy && sudo docker compose -f docker-compose.yml up -d --pull never worker
#
# Then confirm from the worker's own log rather than from this file, and read it in the direction
# that carries signal. With the gate OFF the cron jobs log a warning naming themselves and do no
# work, so that warning's PRESENCE proves the gate is closed - which is what an operator needs to
# confirm today, because the flip is blocked. Its ABSENCE proves nothing on its own: a crashed
# worker, an unregistered cron, a filtered log level or a tick that has not come yet all produce
# the same silence, and nothing logs the value at start.
#JOBTECH_INGEST_ENABLED=true
#
# Grants the Admin role to the account with this address. Read by IdempotentAdminRoleSeeder
# at every Api START and never at registration, so an account registered after the boot that
# opened the gate gets the role on the NEXT restart — the boot in between logs "ingen
# matchande user hittades", which is expected and not a failure. Empty = no assignment.
# The value is a real address and belongs only in deploy/.env on the box; this template and
# the repo are public.
#ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_INITIAL_ADMIN_EMAIL=
